// MTEngineSDLTemplateApp -- TRACKED app build settings.
//
// The app half of the capability channel, and without it the app's own
// translation units would compile with NO capability defines while the engine
// had them -- the two-sources-of-truth shape that produced the httplib ODR bug.
//
// This file is tracked, hand-written and never regenerated. What is FORBIDDEN is
// a baseConfigurationReference pointing at the GENERATED MTEngineCaps.xcconfig,
// whose path carries a capability hash and therefore cannot be tracked. This one
// has no such problem: its only capability content is the consumption line.
//
// It references $(MT_CAPS_DEFINES) and NOT $(MT_CAPS_DEFINES_ENGINE). That
// asymmetry IS the per-target split: measured, a command-line setting reaches
// every target in an invocation and cannot be scoped by the supplier, so the
// split is made here, by which settings each target's xcconfig chooses to read.
// A flag no app guards on stays out of app TUs because this line does not
// mention it.

// MACOS is a platform define, not a capability: mtcaps has no source for it.
GCC_PREPROCESSOR_DEFINITIONS = $(inherited) MACOS $(MT_CAPS_DEFINES) $(MT_APP_DEFAULT_CAPS)

// Fallback for opening this project directly in Xcode with no wrapper run. The
// build then still fails at the agreement check -- that is the designed outcome,
// not a shortfall -- but it fails with a message rather than with a thousand
// errors from a TU compiled with no capability defines at all.
MT_APP_DEFAULT_CAPS = $(MT_APP_DEFAULT_CAPS_$(MT_CAPS_SUPPLIED))
MT_CAPS_SUPPLIED = NO
MT_APP_DEFAULT_CAPS_NO = MT_ENABLE_IMGUI_TEST_ENGINE=1
MT_APP_DEFAULT_CAPS_YES =

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE FFMPEG DYLIBS, and why they are a variable rather than five literals.
//
// A capability that cannot LINK when it is off is not optional. With
// MT_CAP_VIDEO_PLAYBACK=0 the codec script correctly builds no FFmpeg dylibs,
// and OTHER_LDFLAGS naming them by absolute path then fails the link with five
// "no such file or directory" errors. Linux and Windows have always got this
// right -- CMakeLists.txt wraps the link in if(MT_ENABLE_FFMPEG) and
// MTEngineApp.targets has a whole MTCapsFFmpegLinkInputs target -- and only
// Xcode did not, because its link flags are a flat list with no conditionals.
//
// Xcode has no `if`, but it does have variable indirection: $(X_$(Y)) expands Y
// first and then looks up the composed name. That is the same trick
// MT_APP_DEFAULT_CAPS uses above, so this file already relies on it working.
//
// THE EMPTY SUFFIX IS THE UNSET CASE and it must carry the dylibs. FFmpeg's
// capability defaults to 1, so "absent means on" -- an IDE build that never ran
// the pre-action has no MT_ENABLE_FFMPEG at all, and must keep linking exactly
// what it linked before this variable existed.
MT_FFMPEG_LDFLAGS = $(MT_FFMPEG_LDFLAGS_$(MT_ENABLE_FFMPEG))
MT_FFMPEG_LDFLAGS_1 = $(MT_CAPS_LIBS_DIR)/libavutil.dylib $(MT_CAPS_LIBS_DIR)/libavcodec.dylib $(MT_CAPS_LIBS_DIR)/libavformat.dylib $(MT_CAPS_LIBS_DIR)/libswscale.dylib $(MT_CAPS_LIBS_DIR)/libswresample.dylib
MT_FFMPEG_LDFLAGS_0 =
MT_FFMPEG_LDFLAGS_ = $(MT_FFMPEG_LDFLAGS_1)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE OPTIONAL ENGINE ARCHIVES, gated the same way as the FFmpeg dylibs above.
//
// With their capability off the engine neither fetches nor builds these
// archives, so a fresh machine (a CI runner) has none of them and an
// unconditional -l fails the link. The empty suffix (no capability set
// supplied) links them, as FFmpeg does: absent means ON.
MT_CAPS_OPTIONAL_LDFLAGS = $(MT_OPENCV_LDFLAGS) $(MT_LLAMA_CPP_LDFLAGS) $(MT_MBEDTLS_LDFLAGS)

MT_OPENCV_LDFLAGS = $(MT_OPENCV_LDFLAGS_$(MT_ENABLE_OPENCV))
MT_OPENCV_LDFLAGS_1 = -lmt_opencv
MT_OPENCV_LDFLAGS_0 =
MT_OPENCV_LDFLAGS_ = $(MT_OPENCV_LDFLAGS_1)

MT_LLAMA_CPP_LDFLAGS = $(MT_LLAMA_CPP_LDFLAGS_$(MT_ENABLE_LLAMA_CPP))
MT_LLAMA_CPP_LDFLAGS_1 = -lllama_cpp
MT_LLAMA_CPP_LDFLAGS_0 =
MT_LLAMA_CPP_LDFLAGS_ = $(MT_LLAMA_CPP_LDFLAGS_1)

MT_MBEDTLS_LDFLAGS = $(MT_MBEDTLS_LDFLAGS_$(MT_ENABLE_MBEDTLS))
MT_MBEDTLS_LDFLAGS_1 = -lmbedtls_bundle
MT_MBEDTLS_LDFLAGS_0 =
MT_MBEDTLS_LDFLAGS_ = $(MT_MBEDTLS_LDFLAGS_1)

// Where the dependency archives live. LIBRARY_SEARCH_PATHS in the project reads
// this, so it must have a value even in the no-wrapper case above -- an empty
// setting there would silently search the wrong directory rather than fail.
//
// Both real channels override it: the CLI wrapper passes MT_CAPS_LIBS_DIR on
// the xcodebuild command line, and the pre-action writes it into the file
// #include?d at the bottom of this file. This literal is only the fallback, and
// it must agree with mt_caps_lib_dir's own fallback in the engine's
// platform/caps-lib.sh, which is what the acquisition scripts use in the same
// situation. An xcconfig cannot read $MTENGINE_BUILD_ROOT or $XDG_CACHE_HOME,
// so the default location is spelled out.
MT_CAPS_LIBS_DIR = $(HOME)/.cache/mtengine/_deps/standalone/libs

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE IDE CHANNEL.
//
// Written by the scheme's build PRE-ACTION (tools/mtcaps/xcode-preaction.sh) at
// the start of every Xcode build, and OUTSIDE every checkout. It is what makes
// an IDE Build or Archive honour mtengine.caps rather than the engine's
// defaults -- without it, Archive could ship libraries the manifest turns off.
//
// `#include?`, not `#include`: a missing file degrades SILENTLY, so a developer
// who has never run the pre-action still gets a working build (falling back to
// the defaults below, with the in-target backstop deciding whether that is
// acceptable for this manifest).
//
// Measured: a pre-action's write takes effect in the SAME build, and an absolute
// #include? resolves.
//
// A command-line setting still beats this, so the script path is unaffected:
// the wrapper's values win and the two agree anyway.
//
//
// THE PATH IS RELATIVE, and it has to be. Measured: $(HOME) and build settings
// generally do NOT expand inside an #include? path -- only a literal absolute
// path works there, and hardcoding one is both forbidden here and wrong for
// anyone else's machine. A relative path resolves from THIS file's directory,
// which lands on `.mtengine-ide/` beside the five repos: inside none of them,
// and requiring only the sibling layout the whole programme already assumes.
//
// IT MUST COME LAST IN THIS FILE. In an xcconfig a LATER assignment wins, so
// an include placed above the fallback defaults is silently overwritten by
// them -- measured: MT_CAPS_SUPPLIED stayed NO with the include at the top,
// and the IDE build compiled against the defaults while the pre-action had
// written the correct file.
#include? "../../../.mtengine-ide/MTEngineSDLTemplateApp/caps.xcconfig"

